1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,750
Effect boundaries. A doeff program runs in two zones: inside doeff, and outside it.

2
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:12,883
Inside, the handler stack answers each effect, with k, the rest of the program.

3
00:00:12,883 --> 00:00:16,050
Outside, Python's event loop runs your coroutines.

4
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:24,550
Almost every effect stays inside. Await is the one that crosses the line.

5
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:38,550
Here is the page's pipeline: Ask, Spawn, Gather, Wait and Try, and each worker's Get, Put and Tell.

6
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:47,650
An observer handler sees all fifteen effects go by. Each one is answered by a handler inside doeff.

7
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:11,200
All of it runs on one thread, with no event loop at all. Even the tasks are just the scheduler handler's work.

8
00:01:13,050 --> 00:01:18,650
Await is different. Python's await is syntax, and it only runs under an event loop.

9
00:01:19,550 --> 00:01:24,283
await handler hands each coroutine to an event loop on its own thread,

10
00:01:24,283 --> 00:01:28,000
the await bridge, and the scheduler parks the task's k.

11
00:01:28,550 --> 00:01:34,100
While A and B are outside, task C's steps are still answered inside doeff,

12
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:36,800
all before either coroutine returns.

13
00:01:37,350 --> 00:01:41,917
B's coroutine returns at about a hundred milliseconds, and its k resumes.

14
00:01:41,917 --> 00:01:45,800
A follows at two hundred: about two hundred in all, not three.

15
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:54,900
Waiting inside doeff is scheduler suspension: a handler holds the task's k, and resumes it later.

16
00:01:55,450 --> 00:02:00,283
A Wait on a promise another task completes takes under two milliseconds.

17
00:02:00,283 --> 00:02:03,850
A three second Delay on the virtual clock, under one.

18
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:10,000
An Await of a point three second sleep takes three hundred milliseconds of real time,

19
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,900
answered on the bridge thread. Only that wait leaves doeff.

20
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:21,000
Your own handlers keep the boundary strict. Here is one program, Fetch a URL, under two handlers.

21
00:02:21,550 --> 00:02:29,500
from table answers inside doeff. over async needs Python async, so it performs Await itself.

22
00:02:30,050 --> 00:02:36,600
That Await travels outward, so await handler goes outside over async, and scheduled outside both.

23
00:02:39,050 --> 00:02:44,250
Leave await handler out, or put it on the inside, and the Await finds no handler.

24
00:02:46,050 --> 00:02:50,683
So answer domain effects in handlers, cross only for real async work,

25
00:02:50,683 --> 00:02:53,500
and leave task switching to the scheduler.

26
00:02:55,050 --> 00:02:59,400
doeff: effects are answered by handlers, inside doeff.

27
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:04,950
Await is the one door out, to Python's event loop. pip install doeff.
